  "conclusion": "Across two accepted runs per condition, the fixed 120 Hz Galaxy results overlapped the fixed 60 Hz range: missing rVFC callbacks were 3,677-4,284 versus 3,823-3,983, and draw submits were 25.10-29.11 versus 26.42-27.98 per second. Fixed 120 Hz did not consistently reduce the Galaxy live Original rendering deficit.",
  "limitations": [
    "rVFC missedCallbackFrames counts gaps in callback observation relative to the presentedFrames counter; it is not a direct count of physical display scanout drops.",
    "Each run covers a different ten-minute live broadcast interval. Within each run the devices share the same live input interval, but differences between refresh conditions are not randomized paired treatment effects.",
    "POCO retained its normal OS setting. Its draw-submit rate varied in one fixed-120 paired run, so it is a concurrent device control rather than a stable performance reference.",
    "Physical scanout, pixel correctness, subjective picture quality, and audible A/V synchronization were not measured.",
    "All four runs passed the same actual Original request, player-state, Worker lifecycle, autoFilm, trace-drop, settings restoration, tuner, and cleanup validation contracts.",
    "Source filesystem paths and LAN addresses are omitted; SHA-256 values retain traceability to the private archive."
  ]
